<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jixia Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Jixia Xuegong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jixia Academy was a centre of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, capital of the State of Qi, named after the Ji Gate of the city. From the fourth century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> rulers of Qi, notably King Xuan, gave scholars of many schools stipends, houses and titles to debate questions of government and philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, Xunzi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zou-yan">Zou Yan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a> were among those associated with it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jixia-academy.jpg" alt="Jixia Academy" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitsuha · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A8%B7%E4%B8%8B%E5%AD%B8%E5%AE%AE_%E6%B7%84%E5%8D%9A%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A4%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
